Leonard Corporation]] | isbn=9781480393318}}</ref><ref name=mw>{{cite web | url=http://www.marketwired.com/press-release/daniel-powters-bad-day-is-best-selling-digital-song-of-all-time-696356.htm | title=Daniel Powter's "Bad Day" Is Best-Selling Digital Song of All Time | date=July 19, 2006 | publisher=[[Marketwired]] | access-date=December 14, 2014 | archive-date=July 5, 2018 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180705073539/http://www.marketwired.com/press-release/daniel-powters-bad-day-is-best-selling-digital-song-of-all-time-696356.htm | url-status=dead }}</ref> The song is composed in the [[key (music)|key]] of [[E-flat major|E♭ major]] (with the bridge in [[F-sharp major|F♯ major]]), and uses [[Syncopation|syncopated]] [[Sixteenth note|16th-note]] rhythms.<ref name=hal/> According to the sheet music published at Musicnotes.com by Warner Bros., Powter's vocals range from the note of E♭<sub>3</sub> and [[D♭ (musical note)|D♭]]<sub>5</sub>.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.musicnotes.com/sheetmusic/mtd.asp?ppn=MN0067708 | title=Bad Day By Daniel Powter - Digital Sheet Music | publisher=Musicnotes.com | access-date=January 20, 2016| date=September 8, 2008 }}</ref> Its instrumentation differs from "the scores of adolescent thrust-rockers"<ref name=ct>{{cite magazine | author=Taylor, Chuck | date=March 18, 2006 | title=Reviews: Singles |magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]] | publisher=[[Prometheus Global Media]] | volume=118 | issue=11 | page=60 | url=https://books.google.com/books?id=HhYEAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA60}}</ref> and includes, as Powter referred to it, "aggressive" drums.<ref name=cbs/> David Browne of ''[[Entertainment Weekly]]'' said it is: "addressed to anyone who's feeling depressed&nbsp;...&nbsp;but [in contrast] its grand, panoramic arrangement wants to pump you up".<ref>{{cite magazine | url=http://www.ew.com/article/2006/04/10/daniel-powter | title=Daniel Powter | author=Browne, David | date=April 10, 2006 | magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]] | publisher=[[Time Inc.]] | access-date=February 5, 2015}}</ref> Simon Donohue of the ''[[Manchester Evening News]]'' commented its sound "{{sic|seagues}} from boy band banality to [[Foo Fighters]]-style raucous rock".<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.manchestereveningnews.co.uk/whats-on/music/daniel-powter---bad-day-1074079 | title=Daniel Powter – Bad Day (Warner Bros.) | author=Donohue, Simon | date=July 1, 2005 | work=[[Manchester Evening News]] | access-date=February 11, 2015}}</ref> According to Winston Kung of ''[[PopMatters]]'', it is "in tune with the [[zeitgeist]]".<ref name=popm>{{cite web | url=http://www.popmatters.com/review/daniel_powter_daniel_powter/ | title=Daniel Powter: Daniel Powter | date=April 26, 2006 | author=Kung, Wiston | work=[[PopMatters]] | access-date=January 16, 2015}}</ref>

The lyrics of "Bad Day" were said to have a universal appeal by [[Alan Connor]] of ''[[BBC News Magazine]]'' as they have an "everyman breeziness" because the song's subject can be any person going through a bad daytime.<ref name=bbcm>{{cite news | url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/magazine/7518881.stm | title=Secrets of writing a 'wallpaper' hit | author=Connor, Alan | date=July 22, 2008 | work=[[BBC News Magazine]] | publisher=[[BBC]] | access-date=December 19, 2014}}</ref> Stephen Thomas Erlewine from [[AllMusic]] described it as: "a loping, sunny tune that pretty much has the opposite sentiment of its title".<ref name=all/> Although [[About.com]]'s Bill Lamb described its lyrics as having a "reassuring, comforting" tone,<ref>{{cite web | url=http://top40.about.com/od/albums/fr/dpowter.htm | title=Daniel Powter – Daniel Powter |author=Lamb, Bill | work=[[About.com]] | publisher=[[IAC (company)|IAC]] | access-date=January 16, 2015 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160305011400/http://top40.about.com/od/albums/fr/dpowter.htm | archive-date=March 5, 2016}}</ref> Powter said the song "mak[es] fun of self-absorbed and narcissistic people who bitch and gripe".<ref name=times/> He also affirmed: "It's not literally about having a bad day, it's more about not taking yourself too seriously and complaining about trivial things".<ref>{{cite news | url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/cbbcnews/hi/newsid_4730000/newsid_4734400/4734447.stm | title=Newsround speaks to pop star Daniel Powter | author=Boyton, Cristina | work=[[Newsround]] | publisher=[[CBBC (TV channel)|CBBC]] | date=August 1, 2005 | access-date=February 9, 2015}}</ref>

sections.text:
{{Rquote|right|Bitching and griping about nothing. My granddad used to say to me, 'There's better people who are worse off than you,' and I always remember that. It's a song about trying to make people feel better. I'm making fun of you, but at the same time making fun of myself. | Daniel Powter}}

"Bad Day" is a midtempo pop power ballad, performed in a moderate groove and accompanied by a piano. The song is composed in the key of E♭ major (with the bridge in F♯ major), and uses syncopated 16th-note rhythms. According to the sheet music published at Musicnotes.com by Warner Bros., Powter's vocals range from the note of E♭<sub>3</sub> and D♭<sub>5</sub>. Its instrumentation differs from "the scores of adolescent thrust-rockers" and includes, as Powter referred to it, "aggressive" drums. David Browne of Entertainment Weekly said it is: "addressed to anyone who's feeling depressed&nbsp;...&nbsp;but [in contrast] its grand, panoramic arrangement wants to pump you up". Simon Donohue of the Manchester Evening News commented its sound "{{sic|seagues}} from boy band banality to Foo Fighters-style raucous rock". According to Winston Kung of PopMatters, it is "in tune with the zeitgeist".

The lyrics of "Bad Day" were said to have a universal appeal by Alan Connor of BBC News Magazine as they have an "everyman breeziness" because the song's subject can be any person going through a bad daytime. Stephen Thomas Erlewine from AllMusic described it as: "a loping, sunny tune that pretty much has the opposite sentiment of its title". Although About.com's Bill Lamb described its lyrics as having a "reassuring, comforting" tone, Powter said the song "mak[es] fun of self-absorbed and narcissistic people who bitch and gripe". He also affirmed: "It's not literally about having a bad day, it's more about not taking yourself too seriously and complaining about trivial things".

